            <ArticleTitle>Dexamethasone-eluting vascular stents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>296-301</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/bcpt.12056</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Percutaneous transluminal angioplasty (PTA) with stenting is widely used in the treatment of vascular disorders, but restenosis remains a significant problem. Drug-eluting stents (DES) have been developed as an attempt to reduce the intimal response leading to restenosis. Drugs used in DES include mainly immunosuppressive and anti-proliferative compounds. Glucocorticoids are also an interesting possibility for those purposes because they have anti-proliferative effects in vascular smooth muscle cells and down-regulate the production of cytokines and growth factors driving inflammation and fibrosis. In this MiniReview, feasibility and safety of drug-eluting metal and biodegradable vascular stents are discussed with special emphasis on dexamethasone-eluting stents.</AbstractText>
